That's the clean separation.

The most important distinction

I want you to keep this table beside you while developing Veyra.
I would lock these questions as the permanent architectural questions for Veyra.

Subsystem	One question it must answer
core/audio	What is being said?
core/vision	What is visible?
core/documents	How do I turn a file into usable information?
core/context	What information should Veyra know right now?
core/candidate	What do we know about the candidate?
core/conversation	What is happening in the conversation?
core/interview	What interview/question/task is happening?
core/prompts	How should this AI task be instructed?
core/ai	How should this AI request be executed?
core/cloud	Which cloud AI can execute it?
core/models	How do I install, load, and run local models?
core/hardware	What can this computer safely run?
core/reliability	How do we keep operations reliable when things fail?
core/security	How do we protect user data and operations?
core/analytics	What happened, how well did it perform, and why?
database	How do we persist and retrieve durable application state?
desktop	How does Veyra safely access operating-system capabilities?
server	How can Veyra expose optional remote services?
shared	What contracts must independent layers agree on?
client	How does the user see and control Veyra?

That is the boundary system.


